MYSTICMAIL DEMONSTRATION SHELL - SYSOP GUIDE
==========================================

STATUS: DEMO ONLY. This package contains NO real mail service.
It does not read or write Mystic users, JAM bases, NetMail, or email.

PURPOSE
-------
Test the embedded Python interpreter and basic Mystic terminal execution.
The shell presents a fake inbox with local, FTN, and Internet examples.

INSTALLATION (TEST SYSTEM FIRST)
--------------------------------
1. Back up your Mystic configuration and note the current menu settings.
2. Copy mystic/mysticmail.mpy into a TEST Mystic scripts directory.
   Example Windows: E:\mystic\scripts\mysticmail.mpy
   Example Linux: /home/mystic/scripts/mysticmail.mpy
   Adjust paths for YOUR installation.
3. Check your Mystic documentation for the correct Python script invocation,
   embedded interpreter configuration, and any MPY compilation requirement.
   Do not assume that installing Python 3 system-wide changes Mystic's
   embedded interpreter.
4. Add a TEMPORARY sysop-only test menu entry that launches the script
   using the verified Python/MPY command for your Mystic build.
5. Run the test as a logged-in user. Note the displayed Python version,
   whether text renders, whether keyboard input works, and whether Q
   returns cleanly to the Mystic menu.
6. Remove the temporary menu entry when testing is finished.

No Mailgun keys, network service, or backend configuration is needed.

EXPECTED MENU
-------------
1 Unified Inbox (sample)
2 Private Mail (sample)
3 FTN NetMail (sample)
4 Internet Email (sample)
5 Sent (sample)
6 Compose (disabled)
D Interpreter Diagnostics
Q Quit

TROUBLESHOOTING
---------------
- If Python fails to load: check Mystic's interpreter/library configuration,
  the Python bitness, script path, and Mystic build-specific MPY instructions.
- If terminal input behaves oddly: record the exact prompt and key pressed.
- If Mystic reports unsupported syntax: record the Python version and error.
- Do not post real user records, passwords, private mail, or API secrets.

ROLLBACK
--------
Delete the temporary menu entry. Optionally remove the copied demo script.
No data migration or native message-area changes are performed.

FUTURE DESIGN
-------------
MysticMail will eventually use a shared authenticated service for local
private mail, FTN NetMail, and Mailgun Internet email. EchoMail stays native.
Only after end-to-end testing would the SysOp hide the old NetMail menu.
